oks that explore  religions through various lenses  including literature law art race and  culture so first Jennifer Philip and  Priya will offer  brief remarks and three issues that are  interest to most budding authors first  Jennifer will speak about how to prepare  a book manuscript Philip will discuss  how to think about picking a publisher  and Priya will offer advice as to how to  approach a publisher after their brief  remarks we'll begin the Q&amp;A I'll start  with a few questions and then we'll open  it up to the questions from the audience  so Jennifer so this is a very hard  question for me to answer because you  know the longer I work in publishing the  more I realized there's no formulas and  that's part of what makes our job so  exciting but also very challenging and  it really is not a formula about  transforming a dissertation into a book  I'm gonna offer a couple of rough  guidelines but I'll confess that they  are somewhat exaggerated and that  dissertations take all kinds of forms  and you know a book can mean very  different things to very different  people  and I think you know depending on what  kind of audience you're trying to reach  with a book what that transformation  looks like is going to be very very  different  the two basic guidelines I'll offer and  again they're a little bit crude but the  first is that the you know dissertation  is really being written for a handful of  people the book conceivably is being  written for anyone who wants to pick it  up and I I think that there's a range  you know some some people have advisors  who really are encouraging them to write  this for a broader audit the  dissertation for the broader audience  and some people who are publishing their  first book are writing for a very narrow  sort of readership so the degree to the  degree of the transformation will be  determined by those two things where  you're starting we're trying to land but  I think it's one of the biggest  challenges people face w