portant to use so whether price  point is important that's something  that's important to authors and changes  very very rapidly over the years and you  know whether having your book available  as an open access ebook or in paperback  first you know figuring out what's  important to you and then looking at  what different publishers do because it  is going to be different for  every author in every book thank you  very much so let me just ask one more  question and then we'll move on to  individual consultations so my final  question for all panelists is if you  could only give a single piece of advice  to a graduate student or a postdoctoral  fellow hoping to publish his or her work  what would it be I you know I always  think the most important thing is just  write the book that you are most  passionate about and let the chips fall  Beibei because you know they're it's a  very unpredictable process you could  write the most brilliant proposal that's  very publishable but you there just  isn't quite the right publisher or  editor out there and it seems to fall  between cracks books come out and don't  get the attention that people hope for  and you've invested years you know I  think to just do the work that you feel  most deeply inspired to do and usually  that translates into some sort of  success but you can't count on that  success so for you know to feel like  that time and energy that you've put  into the project was was something that  you needed to do for yourself yeah I  agree with that I think but I would say  something I think that Priya was  suggesting as well and have a clear  sentence more practical I mean have a  clearer sense of what's at stake in your  project what you want to say and to whom  you want it to be said and you know in  terms of audience me there's no sort of  right or wrong audience I mean they're  great books that we published that will  reach 500 meters and they're great books  that we publish that will reach 5000 and  they both have a kind of value for the  kind of publish  we do yeah I think what I'm gonna say is  gonna echo both of those comments uh one  of the really difficult things about  being in a situation doesn't early  scholar and trying to revise a book is  that you know there is an obvious answer  to why must my book be written it's  because there are job market concerns  and there's tender issues so you have to  bracket that in your mind and hold it  there you know somewhere and in a  confined space that doesn't overpower  you and and some kind of anxious and  nervous state and try to reconnect with  what why must the book be written from  an intellectual standpoint what is the  really crucial thing that's driving this  process I think everybody gets into  academia because they're excited about  ideas there's a tremendous amount of  pressure that dulls that excitement a  bit so recapturing a sense of wonder and  your project curiosity finding what is  still there and frankly if it's not  still there maybe you could get your  dissertation on quickly and find  something else or cannibalize their  dissertation and write articles for some  places where you're going to be teaching  that's also an acceptable way of finding  a long-term position so take a moment  think about how you want to spend the  next few years of your life and try to  recapture a sense of excitement about it  yeah I say all of those things  but also I think it's really important  to let your personality shine through  and show your passion and why you're  writing this book and why you're  devoting your career to this because at  the end of the day I think publishing is  all about relationships  your relationship with your editor your  relationship with your reader and I  don't want just to read I think someone  use the word robotic a robotic proposal  or manuscript I want to see who you are  and from the very first letter you write  to me when you submit your proposal to  once you  have a finished book I think I want to  see who the author is and you know feel  your presence when I'm reading your book  thank you all  this is amazing advice and thank you for  all the other really useful nuggets of  advice we would like to thank you all  for participating
 

